          body:
            "Pure motor weakness narrows the search to the motor unit (anterior horn cell, motor axon, [[nmj|neuromuscular junction]], muscle) or the corticospinal pathway. Three patterns cover most cases.\n\n[[myopathy|Myopathy]]: symmetric [[proximal-weakness|proximal weakness]] (stairs, chairs, lifting the arms), often neck flexor weakness and a [[waddling-gait|waddling gait]]. Reflexes are preserved until weakness is severe. Wasting comes late. [[creatine-kinase|Creatine kinase]] (CK) is often raised.\n\nNeuromuscular junction disease: weakness that fluctuates and worsens with use ([[fatigability|fatigability]]), with ptosis, diplopia, and [[bulbar|bulbar]] symptoms in myasthenia gravis. Reflexes and CK are normal. (This was the subject of the previous module.)\n\n[[motor-neuron-disease|Motor neuron disease]]: asymmetric weakness that starts focally and spreads, with wasting and [[fasciculation|fasciculations]], and in [[amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis|ALS]] a mixture of [[lmn|lower]] and [[umn|upper motor neuron]] signs, such as brisk reflexes in a wasted limb. Sensation, eye movements, and bladder control are usually spared.",
