          body:
            "[[nerve-conduction-studies|Nerve conduction studies]] and needle [[electromyography|electromyography]] (EMG) test the motor unit directly.\n\n- Sensory nerve responses are normal in myopathy, NMJ disease, and motor neuron disease. A sensory abnormality points toward nerve, root, or plexus instead.\n- Myopathic EMG: small, short, often polyphasic motor unit potentials that are recruited early, because each unit has fewer working fibers and more units must fire for a given force. Inflammatory and necrotizing myopathies also show spontaneous activity (fibrillation potentials).\n- Neurogenic EMG: fibrillations and fasciculations from denervation, and large, long-duration units firing with reduced recruitment, because surviving neurons adopt orphaned fibers. In ALS these changes are found in several regions, including muscles that are not yet weak.\n- NMJ disease: [[repetitive-nerve-stimulation|repetitive nerve stimulation]] shows a decrement in myasthenia gravis and a marked increase after exercise in Lambert-Eaton syndrome.\n- Motor [[conduction-block|conduction block]] with normal sensory responses suggests multifocal motor neuropathy, a treatable ALS mimic.",
