            "The idiopathic inflammatory myopathies are autoimmune muscle diseases. The main types differ in clues and in what to look for next.\n\n- [[dermatomyositis|Dermatomyositis]]: proximal weakness with skin signs, including a violet [[heliotrope-rash|heliotrope rash]] of the eyelids and [[gottron-papules|Gottron papules]] over the knuckles. In adults it is associated with cancer, so age-appropriate cancer screening is part of the workup. Interstitial lung disease and dysphagia also occur.\n- Antisynthetase syndrome (for example anti-Jo-1 antibodies): myositis with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, and cracked skin on the fingers.\n- [[immune-mediated-necrotizing-myopathy|Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy]]: often severe proximal weakness with a very high CK and muscle fiber necrosis with little inflammation on biopsy. Anti-HMGCR antibodies link many cases to statin exposure, and weakness continues after the statin is stopped.\n- [[inclusion-body-myositis|Inclusion body myositis]]: slowly progressive weakness in older adults, often asymmetric, with weak finger flexors and quadriceps, frequent falls, and dysphagia. CK is normal or mildly raised, and it does not respond to immunosuppression.\n\n[[myositis-specific-antibodies|Myositis-specific antibodies]], EMG, muscle MRI, and biopsy define the type. Classification criteria such as the 2017 EULAR/ACR criteria combine these features, but they are designed for research classification rather than to make the diagnosis in one patient.",
