            "[[amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis|Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis]] (ALS) is degeneration of both upper motor neurons (in the motor cortex) and lower motor neurons (in the brainstem and [[anterior-horn|anterior horn]] of the spinal cord). The clinical signature is the combination:\n\n- [[lmn|Lower motor neuron]] signs: weakness, wasting, fasciculations, cramps.\n- [[umn|Upper motor neuron]] signs: brisk or preserved reflexes in wasted muscles, [[spasticity|spasticity]], [[babinski-sign|Babinski sign]], a brisk [[jaw-jerk|jaw jerk]].\n\nOnset is usually focal (a hand, a foot, or speech and swallowing in bulbar onset) and spreads to other regions. Sensation, eye movements, and sphincters are usually spared. Some patients develop cognitive or behavioral change in the frontotemporal spectrum, and [[pseudobulbar-affect|pseudobulbar affect]] can occur.\n\nDiagnostic criteria divide the body into four regions: bulbar, cervical, thoracic, and lumbosacral. The older revised El Escorial criteria grade certainty by how many regions show both UMN and LMN signs. The 2020 [[gold-coast-criteria|Gold Coast criteria]] simplify this: progressive motor impairment, plus UMN and LMN dysfunction in at least one region or LMN dysfunction in at least two regions (by examination or EMG), plus investigations that exclude other diseases.",
