            "Respiratory muscle weakness is the usual cause of death in ALS, so ask about it at every visit: breathlessness lying flat (orthopnea), broken sleep, morning headache, daytime sleepiness, and a weak cough. Measure [[vital-capacity|vital capacity]] (sitting and lying) and other respiratory tests. [[noninvasive-ventilation|Noninvasive ventilation]] improves symptoms and survival. Oxygen alone does not treat hypoventilation and can worsen carbon dioxide retention.\n\nSwallowing problems and weight loss are managed with diet changes and, when needed, a gastrostomy, ideally placed while breathing is still strong enough for the procedure. Care in a multidisciplinary clinic (neurology, respiratory, nutrition, speech and language, therapy, palliative care) is associated with better survival and quality of life.\n\nGiving the diagnosis is a skill in itself. Arrange a private, unhurried meeting with time protected and a support person if the patient wishes. Find out what they already know and want to know, give a warning that serious news is coming, use plain words, pause, and respond to emotion. Be honest about uncertainty in the course, say what can be done (symptom control, breathing and feeding support, research), and arrange early follow-up. Advance care planning, including views on ventilation, is introduced over time rather than all at once.",
